    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (ME/CFS) is a debilitating illness characterized by overwhelming and persistent fatigue and pain. ME/CFS is estimated to effect as many as 2.5 million Americans, with most living undiagnosed due to a lack of specific laboratory tests for ME/CFS and the similarity of symptoms to other prevalent illnesses including Major Depressive Disorder. Although the cause of ME/CFS is unknown, evidence suggests that there may be an autoimmune component and that CD8+ T cells play a role in the disease pathology. Analysis of PBMCs from a cohort of ME/CFS patients via single-cell sequencing has led to the identification of CFS-TCRB1, a T-cell receptor (TCR) sequence having a peptide-MHC specificity shared among many patients in the cohort. This TCR is activated by endogenous, yeast-library, and exogenous peptides, however whether these peptides are relevant to ME/CFS is unknown. Analysis of single-cell sequencing data from a new patient cohort revealed 9 TCRs present in multiple patients which contain CDR3beta motifs that cluster with CFS-TCRB1 or tetramer derived sequences. These sequences were expressed in a-/b- Jurkat T cells and one cell line, CFS-TCR9, was activated by peptides predicted to bind to CFS-TCRB1. The CFS-TCR9 receptor bound with high specificity to soluble HLA-A*02:01 tetramers loaded with CFS-TCRB1 derived peptides. The results of this study support the notion that ME/CFS has an autoimmune component and certain CD8+ T cells that are differentially expressed in some CFS patients recognize certain endogenous and exogenous peptides. Thus, this study provides insight into the disease mechanism of ME/CFS in some patients with which to design diagnostics and treatment regimens.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
